WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.680
The 2017 big-screen adaptation of Stephen
King's novel It is a major success with critics

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.810
and audiences — due in no small part to
the way the movie incorporates the book's

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:14.650
most essential elements while sidestepping
some of its more controversial or uncomfortable

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.650
moments.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.640
These are the most disturbing scenes from
It that were left behind during the long journey

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.320
to theaters.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.240
Patrick Hockstetter's cruelest act

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.730
In the movie, Patrick Hockstetter is hardly
a central figure to the story.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.570
He's clearly a jerk, but he's not nearly as
terrible as he was in the novel.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.729
Patrick is an exceptionally disturbed young
man in the book, and few scenes show that

00:00:35.729 --> 00:00:39.319
more than when he suffocates his baby brother
in his crib.

00:00:39.319 --> 00:00:40.879
Why was this cut for the movie?

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.620
Maybe to save time and narrow the narrative
focus, or...maybe the filmmakers just had

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.280
no interest in getting quite that dark.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.729
Patrick's death scene

00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:55.309
In the 2017 movie, Patrick's death scene is
entirely different than the way it goes down

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:56.309
in the novel.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:59.579
He dies in the Barrens' sewer drain while
stalking the Losers.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:03.269
On paper, however, Patrick's death is a little
more disturbing.

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:07.440
After he and Henry find themselves alone in
the woods, Patrick makes a sexual advance

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.720
on Henry, who ends up leaving...eventually.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.390
Left alone, Patrick is attacked by It in the
form of swarming bugs, a death that matches

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.430
his worst nightmare.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.290
Henry Bowers' home life

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.710
In the movie, Henry Bowers' father is portrayed
as an alcoholic police officer who has no

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.860
qualms about accosting his son in front of
his friends.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.640
But aside from the gun scene, his cruelty
is mostly hinted at.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.820
In the book, however, Henry's home life is
a lot more messed up.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.910
Butch Bowers is a Marine veteran who's developed
psychosis and is verbally and physically abusive

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.140
to his family, including Henry.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.460
The only thing Henry seems to be able to do
right by his father is act upon their shared

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.120
racism against the Hanlon family.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.120
The town blaze

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.180
The 2017 It movie handles Mike's history in
very obscure terms.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.311
We're told his parents died in a gruesome
house fire and that he'd seen them burn, which

00:01:58.311 --> 00:02:03.090
is why he's so haunted by the image of burnt
hands reaching out from the meat store's alley

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.090
entrance.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.920
He's shown as the bearer of some bad news
about the town's history, courtesy of his

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.150
grandfather, but for the most part, the role
of the resident historian of the group is

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.170
given to Ben Hanscom instead.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.129
The book goes into a lot more detail about
Mike's pain.

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:21.310
The notorious town fire referenced in the
movie took place at a club called the Black

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.790
Spot, and his dad was was there the night
it was burned down.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.969
It's also Mike's dad who encourages him to
study the town's sinister history, which is

00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:33.819
how he becomes the librarian — and the only
one to stay in Derry after the rest of the

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:35.219
Losers' Club leaves.

00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:36.879
The death of Eddie Corcoran

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:41.159
Not only is Eddie Corcoran's story one of
the more tragic subplots in the book, but

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:43.340
he didn't even make it into the movie.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.630
A student at the same school as the Losers,
Eddie's stepfather kills his little brother

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.680
with a hammer, which leaves Eddie on the run
for his life.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.299
He ends up in the Barrens where he encounters
It, which takes the form of his recently deceased

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.640
little brother... before turning into a gill
man and decapitating Eddie.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.620
Eddie's first encounter

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:12.659
Eddie Kaspbrak's first encounter with It in
the movie is relatively similar to the novel:

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:17.909
he passes by the creepy house on Neibolt Street
and runs into a leper, which is the culmination

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:21.169
of his worst fear of severe and contagious
illnesses.

00:03:21.169 --> 00:03:23.900
In the book, however, the scene's a little
more adult.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:29.109
Many readers thought Eddie's mother also stunted
his emotional and sexual maturity by demanding

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.579
that all his affection be reserved for her.

00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:36.459
The leper doesn't make fun of his medication;
instead, he offers to perform sexual favors

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:41.670
in exchange for cash, suggesting that Eddie's
been taught to fear his sexuality, most likely

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.599
because of communicable diseases.

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:47.900
That's…a little too heavy to explore in
a single scene, so it's easy to understand

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.409
why it was ditched.

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:51.090
The goon squad

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:55.879
Henry Bowers is shown tumbling down the well
to his possible death in the 2017 movie — and

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:59.949
aside from Patrick, the rest of his bully
crew seems to get out of the movie without

00:03:59.949 --> 00:04:00.949
getting hurt.

00:04:00.949 --> 00:04:05.079
In the book, however, they're all caught by
It in the sewers after chasing the Losers.

00:04:05.079 --> 00:04:09.590
Vic gets his head torn off by the creature,
who appears as Frankenstein's monster, and

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.999
Belch has his face ripped in half after trying
to defend Henry.

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:15.950
So yeah, it's all a lot more graphic in the
book.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.890
The Ritual of Chud

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:22.290
The ending of It certainly simplified things
when it came to the Losers' first banishment

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.500
of the beast.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.600
Once they rescue Beverly from her floating,
comatose state, they're fearless against the

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.600
creature.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.530
After they manage to injure It by striking
its head, the monster slinks into the well

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.740
and the killings stop… for now.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.650
In the book, however, there's a lot more to
it.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:37.850
A whole lot.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:42.470
Bill Denbrough uses an ancient technique called
the Ritual of Chud, which...takes him into

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:47.130
the macroverse where he encounters the universe's
creator, a giant turtle.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.260
Named Maturin.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.720
The turtle informs him that he can only defeat
It in a battle of wills, and Bill weakens

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.170
It enough that the Losers can make a physical
attack.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:05:00.300
It's a much more supernatural and celestial
process than what we see onscreen, and if

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.480
It is any indication, the forthcoming sequel
may also bypass this bonkers aspect of the

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.160
story altogether.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.570
The smoke hole

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:13.270
The movie also completely omitted a book scene
that would probably be regarded as insensitive

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.270
on film.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:18.670
In the scene, Bill uses a Native American
smoke hole ritual in an effort to find out

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:20.780
more about the demon terrorizing Derry.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.490
Bill and Richie end up staying in the hotbox
long enough to realize that It is an evil

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.550
force that's sucking the lifeblood out of
the town.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.780
It's a realization that works just as well
with a rudimentary history lesson, but it's

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:36.720
a clear example of the movie avoiding unnecessary
controversy — which is pretty interesting,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.300
since it's still a movie about kids battling
a child-eating monster.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.770
Of course, as controversial as that would've
been, it's nothing compared to...

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.490
The sewer escape

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.890
This is probably the most infamous scene that
never made it from the book to the screen

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.200
in any version.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.020
In the movie, the Losers have no trouble exiting
the sewer system after their battle with It.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.100
In the book, however, they find that they
can't remember the way out.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.100
The solution?

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.270
At Bev's own suggestion, all six of the boys
have sex with Beverly Marsh in quick succession

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.890
so they can be united again and find their
way back to the Barrens.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:12.780
There's been a lot of debate about this scene
from the book — whether this is belittling,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.230
abusive, or even empowering for Beverly.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:19.060
But even Stephen King himself has since walked
back on its value to the story.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.780
"I wasn't really thinking of the sexual aspect
of it.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.090
The book dealt with childhood and adulthood.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.120
Intuitively, the Losers knew they had to be
together again.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.640
The sexual act connected childhood and adulthood.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.370
It's another version of the glass tunnel that
connects the children's library and the adult

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:35.370
library.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.920
Times have changed since I wrote that scene
and there is now more sensitivity to those

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.920
issues."

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:46.150
Like the 1990 TV miniseries, 2017's It basically
ignores that part of the plot, which saved

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.060
the filmmakers a lot of trouble bringinging
that problematic scene to life — not to

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.910
mention saving a lot of moviegoers from a
pretty awkward conversation once they got

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:55.910
home.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:56.910
Thanks for watching!

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:59.310
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.380
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

